A **telescoping constructor** is a chain of overloaded constructors, each adding one more optional parameter, so callers pick the overload matching how many options they want to set.

```java
new HttpRequest(url);
new HttpRequest(url, timeout);
new HttpRequest(url, timeout, retries);
new HttpRequest(url, timeout, retries, headers);
```

This breaks down fast: a caller who wants headers but not a custom timeout still has to pass a timeout value, and at the call site `new HttpRequest(url, 5000, 3, null)` tells you nothing about which parameter is which without checking the signature.

Takeaway: telescoping constructors scale badly past two or three optional parameters — the call site becomes unreadable and error-prone, since swapped argument order compiles fine and fails silently.

```java
final class HttpRequest {
    private final String url;
    private final int timeout;
    private final Map<String, String> headers;

    private HttpRequest(Builder b) {
        this.url = b.url;
        this.timeout = b.timeout;
        this.headers = Map.copyOf(b.headers);
    }

    static class Builder {
        private String url;
        private int timeout = 3000;
        private Map<String, String> headers = new HashMap<>();

        Builder url(String url) { this.url = url; return this; }
        Builder timeout(int ms) { this.timeout = ms; return this; }
        Builder header(String k, String v) { headers.put(k, v); return this; }

        HttpRequest build() {
            if (url == null) throw new IllegalStateException("url required");
            return new HttpRequest(this);
        }
    }
}
```

Notice `HttpRequest`'s fields are all `final` and its constructor is `private` — the only way to create one is through the builder, and once built it can never be mutated. `build()` is also where the one required field (`url`) gets enforced, since the builder itself can't make a field mandatory at compile time.
